edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073642 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $2x^3 - 6x^2 + 4x= 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073642 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35be9962-fbfd-57f5-906e-41660079063d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073642 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2x.$ We can factor out the $2x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}-3x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-3x+2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x({x}^{2}-3x+2) & =0 \\\\ 2x(x-1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0,$ $x = 1,$ and $x = 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073643 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073643 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $x^3 - x^2 - x = 3x^2 + 4x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073643 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ccefedb4-9e45-56c4-bb11-460ed8fe1793" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073643 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form $P_n(x)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-x & =3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ {x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x & =4x \\\\ {x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $x.$ We can factor out the $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-4x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2 - 4x - 5$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{2}-4x-5) & =0 \\\\ x(x+1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0,$ $x = -1,$ and $x = 5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073644 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073644 #edb/ref 1071 "If $3x^5 + x^3 = 3x^3 - 5x^4,$ then what are the values of $x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073644 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "830e79ed-49ac-537f-aa67-9bfd063bb258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073644 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form $P_n(x)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{5}+{x}^{3} & =3{x}^{3}-5{x}^{4} \\\\ 3{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}+{x}^{3} & =3{x}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $x^3.$ We can factor out the $x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}+5x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $3x^2+5x-2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}+5x-2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(3x-1)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0,$ $x = \\dfrac{1}{3},$ and $x = -2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073745 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073745 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073745 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073745 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a Cubic Equation With No Constant Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073745 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073745 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e25f87c9-3f2e-59a6-ad45-66f0640e68ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186519582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a Cubic Equation by Rearranging" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073746 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2f2d12ff-f760-5c03-88c0-2b7088eb5fb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073747 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073747 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073747 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073747 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073747 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073747 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073747 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073747 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073747 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073747 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a Higher-Order Polynomial Equation With No Constant Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073747 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073747 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3974b5c6-129f-5616-be84-08b6ab779f70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073918 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073918 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073918 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073918 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073918 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073918 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Polynomial Equations Using the GCF" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073918 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186074645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073918 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073918 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073918 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "89e9a31a-99c7-50ea-8e7e-968250c6014a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074015 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186070175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074015 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074015 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074015 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $6{z}^{3}-21{z}^{2}-90z=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074015 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f41bc86-fed5-5013-8ded-0ffbd4097db1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074015 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $3z.$ We can factor out the $3z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}^{3}-21{z}^{2}-90z & =0 \\\\ 3z(2{z}^{2}-7z-30) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $2{z}^{2}-7z-30$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z(2{z}^{2}-7z-30) & =0 \\\\ 3z(2z+5)(z-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=-\\frac{5}{2},$ and $z=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074645 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074645 #edb/ref 1061 18999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074645 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b9bd867-688e-5b33-9e6b-e8c9220a8041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074645 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074646 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074646 #edb/ref 1061 19000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074646 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "98b0bcdc-ffe7-52d4-8923-cedd33b06b00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074646 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074647 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074647 #edb/ref 1061 19001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074647 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f0ed68af-d201-5b9c-b70a-6893d83968fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074647 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074648 #edb/ref 1061 19002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074648 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99f605fb-6db1-565c-9344-a5cac2693c19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074648 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075006 #edb/ref 1064 "A **polynomial equation** is an equation that can be written in the form\n\n$$\nP_n(x)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $P_n(x)$  is a polynomial of degree $n.$\nA **cubic equation** is a polynomial equation of degree $n=3.$\n\nTo solve a polynomial equation with no constant term, we factor the polynomial part using the greatest common factor.\nOnce we've done that, we can apply the zero product rule to the resulting factors.\n\nFor instance, consider the cubic equation $x^3-x=0.$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $x.$ We can factor out the $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-1$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x+1)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, we conclude that the solutions are ${\\color{red}x=0},$ ${\\color{blue}x = -1},$ and ${\\color{purple}x=1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075006 #edb/ref 1065 3398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075006 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "340ec319-ea6c-5434-a216-6a4b8802c426" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075006 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233206 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233206 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114111" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233206 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233206 #edb/ref 1071 "If $3{h}^{5}-32{h}^{4}-4{h}^{3}=16{h}^{3}-13{h}^{5},$ then what are the values of $h?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233206 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d6db630c-4e99-5e1f-8d98-caea7db3fdf0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233206 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(t)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{h}^{5}-32{h}^{4}-4{h}^{3} & =16{h}^{3}-13{h}^{5} \\\\ 16{h}^{5}-32{h}^{4}-4{h}^{3} & =16{h}^{3} \\\\ 16{h}^{5}-32{h}^{4}-20{h}^{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $4{h}^{3}.$ We can factor out the $4{h}^{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{h}^{5}-32{h}^{4}-20{h}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 4{h}^{3}(4{h}^{2}-8h-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $4{h}^{2}-8h-5$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{h}^{3}(4{h}^{2}-8h-5) & =0 \\\\ 4{h}^{3}(2h+1)(2h-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $h=0,$ $h=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and $h=\\frac{5}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234312 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234312 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71778" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234312 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234312 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the equation $5{z}^{3}-35{z}^{2}+50z=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234312 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de2e4bb1-71bc-5d04-acd2-ad307f10ba69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234312 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5z.$ We can factor out the $5z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}^{3}-35{z}^{2}+50z & =0 \\\\ 5z({z}^{2}-7z+10) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${z}^{2}-7z+10$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z({z}^{2}-7z+10) & =0 \\\\ 5z(z-2)(z-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $z=0,$ $z=2,$ and $z=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431362 #edb/ref 1080 "w=-\\frac{3}{2},0,1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431362 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431362 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09e9fedc-4d5e-5788-be94-10e0f727eb89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431364 #edb/ref 1080 "w=-2,0,\\frac{3}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431364 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431364 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b30b2b8-aa8f-5c31-b7e3-bc987c2700fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431384 #edb/ref 1080 "z=-6,\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431384 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431384 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5059480d-49b5-5e4a-bd13-82ce2b77ee01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431387 #edb/ref 1080 "z=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{5}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431387 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431387 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5713fbbf-27f4-5c95-b6fd-bfb010b612f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431392 #edb/ref 1080 "z=-\\frac{5}{2},\\,0,\\,3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431392 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431392 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6b8251d7-0c1c-57d3-82c1-96e020e790ba" #edb/