6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431452 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431452 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114116" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431452 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431452 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the roots of the polynomial $p(w)=60{w}^{6}-105{w}^{5}+45{w}^{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431452 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af7e750a-c93b-50d4-aa96-76ae41484ef4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431452 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the roots of the polynomial, we must solve the equation $p(w)=0,$ that is\n\n$$\n60{w}^{6}-105{w}^{5}+45{w}^{4}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $15{w}^{4}.$ We can factor out the $15{w}^{4}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{w}^{4}(4{w}^{2}-7w+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $4{w}^{2}-7w+3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{w}^{4}(4{w}^{2}-7w+3) & =0 \\\\ 15{w}^{4}(4w-3)(w-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $w=0,$ $w=\\frac{3}{4},$ and $w=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495502 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186496339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495502 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186496342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495502 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186496359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495502 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186496377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495502 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186496384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495502 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495502 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Polynomial Equations Using the GCF" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495502 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186496359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495502 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495502 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495502 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "94a260c5-b12